{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"tool_nTGA0R7HvkfZAPHe3etnf4NH","content":"360\t`eval` is a new compilation request (`yang.antlr.md` 8.6) sharing the caller's\n361\tscope, so it forces the dynamic-scope profile on its function. It is refused\n362\twith `:yang.php/unsupported :eval` until its slice (P9).\n363\t\n364\t## 4. The reference, array and object lifecycle\n365\t\n366\tPHP's observable aliasing (references, copy-on-write separation that unwraps\n367\tunshared references, destructor timing) is defined by the Zend engine's\n368\toperations, so this model is derived from them, not from an abstraction. Each\n369\trow names the Zend handler it maps to; each row is normative and is confirmed\n370\tagainst the pinned PHP CLI before the slice that claims it lands (the blocking\n371\trule, section 17.3).\n372\t\n373\t### 4.1 What the engine does\n374\t\n375\t- A **zval** is a typed slot in a CV, a hashtable bucket, a property table or\n376\t  a temporary. Refcounted kinds carry a header: strings, arrays\n377\t  (`zend_array`), objects, resources and references (`zend_reference\n378\t  {refcount, val}`).\n379\t- **Copying a zval** (`$b = $a`, `SEND_VAR`, `RETURN`, the `FE_RESET_R`\n380\t  temporary, array-literal elements) copies the slot and `GC_ADDREF`s the\n381\t  pointee: for an array, the **hashtable**, never its elements. Literal arrays\n382\t  are immutable and uncounted; the first write duplicates.\n383\t- **Writing into an array** goes through a write-address fetch (`FETCH_DIM_W`,\n384\t  `FETCH_OBJ_W`, `FETCH_DIM_RW`): the container is separated\n385\t  (`SEPARATE_ARRAY`, `zend_array_dup`) when its refcount is above 1, a missing\n386\t  key is auto-vivified, and the element's address is returned. `$a[k] = v`,\n387\t  `$r =& $a[k]`, a by-reference `f($a[k])`, `foreach ($a as &$v)` and `$a[] =\n388\t  v` all use this path, separating each level as reached.\n389\t- **`zend_array_dup`** copies buckets; a reference with refcount 1 whose value\n390\t  is not the array being duplicated is **unwrapped** in the copy (the copy\n391\t  holds the value, `ADDREF`ed); any other refcounted element is `ADDREF`ed;\n392\t  the source is unchanged; registered iterator positions are duplicated onto\n393\t  the copy.\n394\t- **`ASSIGN`** dereferences a reference destination, acquires the new value,\n395\t  stores it, then destroys the old one. **`ASSIGN_REF`** fetches the source\n396\t  for write, does nothing when source and destination are the same zval, wraps\n397\t  a non-reference source in place, `ADDREF`s the reference, stores it, then\n398\t  destroys the old destination content: acquisition precedes release.\n399\t- **`UNSET_CV`** `DELREF`s whatever the CV holds; unset fetches separate but\n400\t  never vivify. **Function exit** releases every CV, on normal return and on\n401\t  unwinding.\n402\t- **Objects** are handles: property writes do not separate; the object is\n403\t  refcounted and finalized at zero; cycles are reclaimed later by the cycle\n404\t  collector.\n405\t\n406\t### 4.2 The substrate mapping\n407\t\n408\tEvery Zend refcounted kind whose count is **observable** becomes a heap cell\n409\twith an explicit `:rc`; kinds whose count is unobservable stay plain values.\n410\t\n411\t+-------------------------+-------------------------+--------------------------+\n412\t| Zend kind               | Substrate               | Why                      |\n413\t+=========================+=========================+==========================+\n414\t| string                  | plain value             | immutable semantics;     |\n415\t|                         |                         | separation never         |\n416\t|                         |                         | observable               |\n417\t+-------------------------+-------------------------+--------------------------+\n418\t| array transitively      | plain persistent        | duplicating such a table |\n419\t| holding no reference,   | ordered map, stored     | is observable only       |\n420\t| instance handle or      | directly in its holder  | through unwrapping, and  |\n421\t| object handle           |                         | there is nothing inside  |\n422\t|                         |                         | to unwrap or release;    |\n423\t|                         |                         | copy-on-write is free    |\n424\t+-------------------------+-------------------------+--------------------------+"}
{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"tool_keAT7kBmATqpgzdJB4qBEXFB","content":"2110\t|       | test programs in the new corpus, | = 1; echo $x;`; every legacy test |\n2111\t|       | the demo on checked-in CST       | program passes with corrected     |\n2112\t|       | packets,                         | expectations; the demo runs its   |\n2113\t|       | `src/dev/subsystems.edn`         | samples parserless in the         |\n2114\t|       | retargeted, `yang/php.cljc`      | browser; no reference to the      |\n2115\t|       | deleted, the compiler revision   | legacy `yang.php` namespace       |\n2116\t|       | removed from the standard        | remains                           |\n2117\t|       | catalog                          |                                   |\n2118\t+-------+----------------------------------+-----------------------------------+\n2119\t| P9    | builtin surface slices (strings  | each a corpus group with PHP      |\n2120\t|       | II, arrays II, JSON, math II),   | parity; `eval` shares the         |\n2121\t|       | `$$`, `extract`, `compact` and   | caller's symbol table; closes     |\n2122\t|       | the dynamic scope, `eval` as a   | corners C5, C6 (prefer-ref), C11, |\n2123\t|       | compilation request              | C12, C13                          |\n2124\t+-------+----------------------------------+-----------------------------------+\n2125\t\n2126\tDeferred: `preg_*` (a portable regex engine), `mb_*`, `serialize`, Reflection,\n2127\tSPL data structures, `DateTime` beyond the time effect, sessions, PDO and\n2128\tevery `ext/*` surface (bound per ADR 0004), return by reference, `goto`, cycle\n2129\tcollection (corner C1).\n2130\t\n2131\t## 16. The yang.core lift enumeration\n2132\t\n2133\tBeyond L1 to L4 (`yang.typescript.md` section 17), PHP requires nothing new.\n2134\tIt exercises `yang.core.stage` (the ok-check on `:yang.cst/ok`),\n2135\t`yang.core.lower` (the name supply and diagnostics with the `:yang.php`\n2136\tnamespace), `yang.core.module` (`module-symbol` with prefix `phpm`,\n2137\t`declare-free-name`), `yang.core.publication` (with `php/imported-unit-names`\n2138\tfrom the literal `include` edges and the class map; no binder-table hook), and\n2139\t`yang.core.prelude` (the emitters, and `admit` with `host-names` adding the\n2140\tbyte-string `data` exports). An optional `:dynamic-scope` helper for the\n2141\tsymbol-table dict is the one candidate addition: Python's\n2142\t`py/global-get`/`py/global-set` generalize to `yang.core`\n2143\t`dict-scope-get`/`dict-scope-set` if lifted; otherwise PHP copies them.\n2144\t`yang.core.names/canonicalize` and `yang.core.claims` are not used.\n2145\t\n2146\t## 17. Support profile and known engine-fidelity corners\n2147\t\n2148\t### 17.1 The support-profile statement\n2149\t\n2150\tThe `yang.php` runtime profile claims **exactly the rows of the transition\n2151\ttable** (section 4.4, as consolidated here) with their fixture sets, verified\n2152\tagainst the pinned PHP 8.3 CLI on the three fixture sources, plus the slice\n2153\tbehaviour of section 15. Every Zend behaviour outside those rows is listed\n2154\tbelow as a named corner with status **restricted** (behaves differently,\n2155\tpinned by a departure fixture) or **unsupported** (refused with a diagnostic),\n2156\tthe deviation it implies, and the slice that would close it. The profile\n2157\tclaims nothing about a corner beyond what its fixture pins. This is the\n2158\tproject's standing pattern (C3's recorded departures, C2's finalization\n2159\trestriction, 8.5.6's refusal postures, the known-gaps ledger): an honest\n2160\tfragment with tracked gaps, not a claim of Zend fidelity.\n2161\t\n2162\tNone of the corners is required by the Phase 3 exit criteria (section 1) or by\n2163\tthe C4 import machinery; where a corner touched one of those it was moved into\n2164\tthe table during the loop (typed-reference sources, destructor timing of\n2165\tdiscarded temporaries, unset without vivification). The corner table is the\n2166\tknown-gaps ledger's PHP section, row for row, owner seat `yang.php` (owner\n2167\tdecision B8.11).\n2168\t\n2169\t### 17.2 Known engine-fidelity corners\n2170\t\n2171\t+---------------------+-------------------------------------+------------------+\n2172\t| Corner              | Engine behaviour and implied        | Status; closing  |\n2173\t|                     | deviation                           | slice            |\n2174\t+=====================+=====================================+==================+\n2175\t| C1 cycle collector  | Engine: `gc_collect_cycles`         | restricted; a    |\n2176\t|                     | reclaims reference cycles and runs  | cycle-collection |\n2177\t|                     | their destructors at collection     | slice after the  |\n2178\t|                     | time. Here: cyclic garbage lives    | reclamation work |\n2179\t|                     | until shutdown and its destructors  | (B8.12)          |\n2180\t|                     | run in the row-22 phase;            |                  |\n2181\t|                     | `gc_collect_cycles()` returns 0;    |                  |\n2182\t|                     | `gc_enable` and `gc_disable` are    |                  |\n2183\t|                     | no-ops                              |                  |\n2184\t|                     | Evidence: the oracle-only fixtures  |                  |\n2185\t|                     | `:oracle-cycle-release-gc`          |                  |\n2186\t|                     | (collected), `:oracle-cycle-        |                  |\n2187\t|                     | release-memory` (leaks) and `:ref-  |                  |\n2188\t|                     | cycle-unset-terminates` (done)      |                  |\n2189\t|                     | close the P4 plan's R2 condition    |                  |\n2190\t|                     | (a) as a known gap.                 |                  |\n2191\t+---------------------+-------------------------------------+------------------+\n2192\t| C2 object id reuse  | Engine: `spl_object_id` and `#n`    | restricted; none |\n2193\t|                     | ids are reused after free. Here:    | planned          |\n2194\t|                     | ids are fresh ordinals, never       |                  |\n2195\t|                     | reused                              |                  |\n2196\t+---------------------+-------------------------------------+------------------+\n2197\t| C3 weak references  | Engine: `WeakReference` and         | unsupported;     |\n2198\t|                     | `WeakMap` observe collection. Here: | after C1         |\n2199\t|                     | refused                             |                  |\n2200\t+---------------------+-------------------------------------+------------------+\n2201\t| C4 `ArrayAccess` by | Engine: `offsetGet` returning by    | restricted; P4-b |\n2202\t| reference,          | reference; \"Indirect modification   |                  |\n2203\t| `&__get`, indirect  | of overloaded element/property\"     |                  |\n2204\t| modification        | notices. Here: results are values;  |                  |\n2205\t|                     | writes to them are notices with no  |                  |\n2206\t|                     | effect                              |                  |\n2207\t+---------------------+-------------------------------------+------------------+\n2208\t| C5 string offset    | Engine: `$s[i] = 'x'`, padding past | restricted; P9   |\n2209\t| writes              | the end, negative offsets, the      | (strings II)     |\n2210\t|                     | `$s[] =` `Error`, multi-byte        |                  |\n2211\t|                     | warnings. Here: row 3 specifies     |                  |\n2212\t|                     | only reads and single-byte in-range |                  |\n2213\t|                     | writes                              |                  |\n2214\t+---------------------+-------------------------------------+------------------+\n2215\t| C6 internal         | Engine: `sort`, `array_push`,       | restricted; P2   |\n2216\t| by-reference        | `preg_match`'s `&$m`, prefer-ref    | (core), P9       |\n2217\t| parameters          | `array_multisort`, the \"Only        | (prefer-ref)     |\n2218\t|                     | variables should be passed by       |                  |\n2219\t|                     | reference\" notice. Here: each       |                  |"}
